 <h2>Overview</h2>
</div>
    <div class="text-container"> 
        
<p> 
Alessandro Twombly’s painting does not present itself as image, but as field.
<br>A field in which form is not yet resolved, but in the process of becoming:
<br>a threshold where life begins to distinguish itself, without yet having separated.
<br>What emerges is not a completed emotion, but its genesis.
<br>An originary movement in which different forces — organic and mineral, fluid and dense — encounter one
<br>another, brush against one another, at times hold one another in suspension.
<br>To observe these works is to enter a temporality of formation:
<br>not what is, but what is happening.
<br>It is as though matter preserves a memory that precedes form.
<br>A memory belonging to an older mode of feeling, inscribed within the very structure of the living that
<br>exists in all things.
<br>In the sculptures, this process condenses and becomes manifest.
<br>Some forms stretch upward in an unstable verticality, like emergent forces seeking their own limit.
<br>Others gather into forms that evoke mineral presences, as though they were custodians of the memory of
<br>primordial life — almost coral-like outcroppings, traces akin to fossils of a passage:
<br>presences that have passed through life and preserve its silent resonance.
<br>This is not representation, but transition.
<br><br>–Marco Cestari   </p>
           
                 
           
